  ├──────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┤
  │ Durability   │ O_DSYNC on open           │ Direct I/O mode        │
  ├──────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┤
  │ Filesystem   │ ZFS required              │ ZFS recommended        │
  └──────────────┴───────────────────────────┴────────────────────────┘
  Remaining Challenges PostgreSQL Identified

  1. Scatter/gather limitations - FreeBSD AIO can't do aio_readv(iovcnt=16) with direct DMA in a single operation
  2. Syscall ratio - io_uring can approach 0 syscalls per I/O (SQPOLL); AIO is ~2-3 per I/O
  3. UFS serialization - concurrent writes serialize per-file on UFS
  4. macOS limits - restrictive AIO limits make it less useful there

  ---
  Verdict

  Your approach is validated by PostgreSQL's production implementation. The key insight both projects share: FreeBSD AIO + kqueue + ZFS provides a security-conscious alternative to io_uring that's been battle-tested for 25+ years.

  The syscall overhead (~2-3 per I/O vs ~0 for io_uring SQPOLL) is the main performance tradeoff, but for security-sensitive deployments where io_uring is disabled, this is the correct path.

