  PostgreSQL AIO Architecture - Deep Dive

  Key Discovery: No posix_aio Backend Exists Yet!

  The PostgreSQL source code in this repo has three AIO methods:
  ┌───────────────────┬────────────────────────┬───────────────┐
  │       File        │         Method         │   Platforms   │
  ├───────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ method_sync.c     │ Synchronous fallback   │ All           │
  ├───────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ method_worker.c   │ Thread pool simulation │ All (default) │
  ├───────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ method_io_uring.c │ Linux io_uring         │ Linux 5.1+    │
  └───────────────────┴────────────────────────┴───────────────┘
  There is NO method_posix_aio.c! The wiki pages and Thomas Munro's talks describe it as planned/in-development, but it hasn't landed in this version.

  ---
  PostgreSQL's Pluggable AIO Design

  The IoMethodOps interface (aio_internal.h:260-331) defines how to add a new backend:

  typedef struct IoMethodOps
  {
      /* Properties */
      bool wait_on_fd_before_close;

      /* Global initialization */
      size_t (*shmem_size)(void);
      void   (*shmem_init)(bool first_time);
      void   (*init_backend)(void);

      /* IO handling */
      bool (*needs_synchronous_execution)(PgAioHandle *ioh);
      int  (*submit)(uint16 num_staged_ios, PgAioHandle **staged_ios);
      void (*wait_one)(PgAioHandle *ioh, uint64 ref_generation);
  } IoMethodOps;

  How method_worker.c Works (What FreeBSD Uses Today)

  This is the thread pool simulation approach:

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    Backend Process                          │
  │  pgaio_io_acquire() → pgaio_io_start_readv()               │
  │            │                                                │
  │            ▼                                                │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐                   │
  │  │  PgAioWorkerSubmissionQueue         │                   │
  │  │  (shared memory ring buffer)        │                   │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────┘                   │
  │            │                                                │
  └────────────│────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
               ▼
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                IO Worker Process (1 of N)                   │
  │                                                             │
  │  1. LWLockAcquire(AioWorkerSubmissionQueueLock)            │
  │  2. pgaio_worker_submission_queue_consume()                │
  │  3. pgaio_io_reopen(ioh)  ← re-open FD in worker process   │
  │  4. pgaio_io_perform_synchronously(ioh) ← BLOCKING pread() │
  │  5. pgaio_io_process_completion(ioh, result)               │
  │                                                             │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Key implementation details from method_worker.c:

  - Submission queue: Shared memory ring buffer (sqes[64])
  - Worker fanout: Each woken worker wakes 2 more (IO_WORKER_WAKEUP_FANOUT = 2)
  - FD reopening: Workers must reopen files via pgaio_io_reopen() since FDs are process-local
  - Synchronous fallback: IOs referencing local memory (PGAIO_HF_REFERENCES_LOCAL) run synchronously in the issuing backend

  How method_io_uring.c Works (Linux Only)

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    Backend Process                          │
  │                                                             │
  │  PgAioUringContext (per-backend):                          │
  │  ├── completion_lock (LWLock)                              │
  │  └── io_uring_ring                                         │
  │            │                                                │
  │            ▼                                                │
  │  io_uring_get_sqe() → io_uring_prep_readv() → io_uring_submit()
  │            │                                                │
  │            ▼ (kernel handles I/O asynchronously)           │
  │                                                             │
  │  io_uring_wait_cqes() → io_uring_peek_batch_cqe()          │
  │  └── pgaio_io_process_completion(ioh, cqe->res)            │
  │                                                             │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Key features:
  - One io_uring per backend (lines 86-98) - created in postmaster, inherited by children
  - Cross-backend completion: Any backend can drain another's completions (via completion_lock)
  - IOSQE_ASYNC hint: For buffered I/O with many in-flight ops, tells kernel to use worker threads (lines 441-442)
  - Shared memory optimization: Uses io_uring_queue_init_mem() on kernel 6.5+ to avoid per-ring mmap overhead

